// Stories for the matrix: STORIES["<civ id>:<activity>"] = "…". See cultures/README.md.
Object.assign(STORIES, {
  // ── Egyptians ──
  "egyptians:hunt": "In the papyrus marshes of the Delta, a nobleman stands in a light reed skiff, throwing stick raised, his wife and daughter beside him and a trained cat leaping at the birds. Nebamun had this scene painted in his Theban tomb around 1350 BCE. Hunting fowl and hippopotamus in the reeds was more than sport: it was order defeating chaos, a joy the dead hoped to keep forever.",
  "egyptians:battle": "Kadesh, 1274 BCE. Ramesses II, young and overconfident, camps ahead of his army when the Hittite chariots of Muwatalli burst across the Orontes and scatter the Division of Ra. The pharaoh rallies his guard and charges again and again until the Ptah division arrives. Neither side truly won, but Ramesses carved his lone stand on temple walls from Abu Simbel to Karnak, the hero of his own poem.",
  "egyptians:gather": "When the flood withdraws, the black earth of Kemet lies wet and soft. Farmers scatter emmer and barley and drive sheep across the fields to tread the seed in. By spring the scribes arrive with measuring cords to count the harvest and set the tax. Reapers sing work songs as their flint sickles cut, and the first sheaf is offered to Renenutet, the cobra goddess of the granary.",
  "egyptians:ritual": "At Thebes, during the Beautiful Festival of the Valley, priests carry the barque of Amun across the river on their shoulders. People write questions on ostraca, a yes and a no, and wait as the god's boat dips toward one answer. Should I trust this man? Will my son recover? The oracle of Amun decided land disputes and appointed officials; the silence before the boat moves is the holiest moment.",
  "egyptians:rain": "Rain almost never falls on Egypt; what everyone waits for is the river. In early summer the Dog Star Sopdet rises with the sun before dawn, and priests at the nilometer of Elephantine read the stone steps every day. Will the water rise to sixteen cubits, enough for plenty? Too little means hunger; too much, ruin. The whole land holds its breath for Hapi.",
  "egyptians:flood": "The inundation is no disaster here but the year's great blessing. Hapi, plump and blue-green, crowned with papyrus, pours the river over the fields and leaves behind the black silt that gives Kemet its name. Villages become islands, work stops, and people sail, feast and labor on the pharaoh's building projects. Hymns to the Nile say: when he rises, the land rejoices and every belly is glad.",
  "egyptians:wind": "Out of the southern desert comes the khamsin, the hot spring wind that blows for days. Sand blots out the sun over Thebes, stings the eyes of fishermen and buries the edges of the fields. Egyptians linked the red desert and its storms to Seth, the god of confusion and strength, who also stood at the prow of Ra's boat to spear the serpent Apep each night.",
  "egyptians:fire": "Imagined: a lamp tips over in a crowded quarter of Deir el-Medina, the workmen's village of the royal tombs. Mudbrick walls hold, but palm-frond roofs and linen burn quickly, and neighbors pass jars of water hand to hand down the narrow lane. Afterward the scribe of the tomb records the loss on a limestone flake, and the women ask Taweret and Bes to guard the hearth.",
  "egyptians:thunder": "Thunder is rare in Egypt and it belongs to Seth, whose voice roars in the sky. In the Tempest Stela, set up at Karnak about 1550 BCE by King Ahmose, a terrible storm sweeps Thebes: darkness, rain, a noise louder than the crowds, tombs collapsed and bodies washed out. The king restores the temples and the land, showing that pharaoh puts the world back in order.",
  "egyptians:birth": "A mother crouches on two birth bricks painted with the face of Meskhenet, who will also decide the child's fate. Midwives chant spells, an ivory wand carved with Taweret and Bes is drawn around the room to keep demons out. Many children did not survive their first year. But who knows what this one may become: a scribe, a priest, or one day a pharaoh like Hatshepsut.",
  "egyptians:death": "Seventy days after death, the mummy of the scribe Ani stands upright at the door of his tomb. A priest in a jackal mask holds it while another touches its mouth with an adze in the Opening of the Mouth, so the dead may eat and speak again. Professional mourners throw dust on their heads and wail. In the Hall of Two Truths his heart will be weighed against Maat's feather.",

  // ── Babylonians ──
  "babylonians:hunt": "Imagined: Babylonian hunters wade into the reed thickets of the southern marshes, nets strung between boats to catch waterfowl and wild boar flushed by dogs. Lions still prowled the river country, and killing one was a royal duty, proof the king protected his flock. A hunter returning with a boar pays part of it to the temple, then sells the rest in the market of Babylon.",
  "babylonians:battle": "Around 1763 BCE Hammurabi of Babylon dams the waters of the Tigris, then releases them to break the walls of Larsa, where old King Rim-Sin has ruled for sixty years. One by one Eshnunna, Mari and Larsa fall. A small city-state becomes an empire, and on a basalt stele Hammurabi presents himself not just as a conqueror but as the shepherd who brings justice to the land.",
  "babylonians:gather": "Along the canals, date palms stand in rows, and in the season of harvest men climb the trunks with ropes to cut the heavy clusters. Hammurabi's laws set rules for gardeners who share the dates with owners. Barley fills the temple granaries of Marduk, and scribes press the tallies into wet clay. A good year means bread and beer for every household in the city.",
  "babylonians:ritual": "A baru diviner slaughters a perfect lamb and opens it before the sun god Shamash and the storm god Adad. He studies the liver, its marks and folds, comparing them with clay models and long tablets of omens. If this sign appears, the king will triumph. Kings did not march, build or marry without asking. The lamb's liver was a tablet on which the gods wrote.",
  "babylonians:rain": "In southern Mesopotamia rain is scarce, and life depends on canals fed by the Euphrates. Farmers watch the river rise with the snowmelt from distant mountains, and astronomers on the ziggurat watch the stars, writing every night in their Astronomical Diaries: the moon, the planets, the weather, the price of barley. Waiting for change, they recorded the sky for over six centuries.",
  "babylonians:flood": "On the eleventh tablet of the Epic of Gilgamesh, Utnapishtim tells how the gods decided to drown humankind. Ea whispered a warning through a reed wall: tear down your house, build a ship. For six days and seven nights the storm raged, and even the gods cowered like dogs. When it ended, he released a dove, a swallow, then a raven that did not return.",
  "babylonians:wind": "In the Enuma Elish, the creation poem recited at the Babylonian New Year, Marduk faces the sea monster Tiamat armed with seven winds: the evil wind, the whirlwind, the hurricane. When she opens her mouth to swallow him, he drives the winds into her body and splits her in two to make heaven and earth. A great storm is the god's weapon, terrible but creating order.",
  "babylonians:fire": "In 689 BCE the Assyrian king Sennacherib, enraged at Babylon's rebellions, sacks the city, burns its houses and temples, and diverts the canals to flood the ruins. He boasts that he made it like a meadow. Babylonians mourned their city, and Marduk's statue was carried off to Assyria. A decade later Sennacherib's own son Esarhaddon began rebuilding it, hoping to calm the gods.",
  "babylonians:thunder": "Adad, lord of storm, rides his bull and holds lightning forks in his hand. When thunder rolls over Babylon, scribes consult the omen series Enuma Anu Enlil: if Adad thunders in this month, the harvest will prosper, or the enemy will come. Thunder is not random; it is a message, and the king's scholars write letters explaining what it means for him.",
  "babylonians:birth": "Babylonian mothers feared Lamashtu, the demoness who stole infants and brought fever. Around the birthing room hung amulets of Pazuzu, a demon with a dog's face, chosen to drive her away. The midwife calls on the goddess Ninhursag. Will this child become a scribe, learning cuneiform at the tablet house, or a merchant on the river? The family prays it simply lives.",
  "babylonians:death": "The dead go down to the House of Dust, Irkalla, where they eat clay and wear feathers. Babylonians buried their kin beneath the floors of their homes, and each month at the kispu ritual the family poured water and set bread for them, calling their names. A ghost without offerings wanders and troubles the living. Remembering the dead is a household duty, not a single day.",

  // ── Assyrians ──
  "assyrians:hunt": "The palace of Ashurbanipal at Nineveh, around 645 BCE: lions are released from cages into an arena ringed by soldiers. The king, in his chariot, fires arrows and finishes them with a spear. The British Museum reliefs show a dying lioness dragging her paralyzed legs. It was a royal ritual: the king, protecting the land against chaos. The carvers gave the lions more pity than they perhaps intended.",
  "assyrians:battle": "In 701 BCE Sennacherib besieges Lachish in Judah. Assyrian engineers build a siege ramp of stones; battering rams roll up it under covering archers, while defenders hurl torches down. The city falls. Reliefs at Nineveh show families walking into exile with their belongings. The Assyrian army was the most organized of its age, and it wanted every visitor to the palace to see this.",
  "assyrians:gather": "Imagined: in the rain-watered plains around Assur, farmers cut barley with bronze sickles while a royal official watches to take his share for the army. Captives resettled from distant lands work beside them, speaking Aramaic. Ashurnasirpal II boasted of planting gardens at Nimrud with trees and seeds collected on his campaigns, and the harvest feeds the next season of war.",
  "assyrians:ritual": "When a lunar eclipse threatened the life of King Esarhaddon, Assyrian scholars set a substitute king on the throne: a commoner given royal robes, while the real king hid as a farmer. For up to a hundred days the omen could fall on the stand-in. Letters to the king show his fear and the scholars' careful reassurance that the danger would pass.",
  "assyrians:rain": "Northern Assyria relies on rain, not canals. When the winter rains fail, the grain withers, and letters from provincial governors reach the king: no rain has fallen, the people are hungry. Some researchers think a long drought helped weaken the empire in its last decades. The priests ask Adad for rain; the farmers watch the clouds come over the Zagros and pass.",
  "assyrians:flood": "In 612 BCE the Medes and Babylonians besiege Nineveh. A later tradition, echoed in the book of Nahum, says the river gates were opened and the palace dissolved. Whether a flood breached the walls or not, Nineveh fell that year and the great empire collapsed within a generation. For the Assyrians, it was an end of the world.",
  "assyrians:wind": "Imagined: a dust storm rolls out of the Syrian steppe while an Assyrian army is on the march. Soldiers wrap their faces, horses turn their tails to the wind, and the chariot column halts. The scribe who keeps the campaign record will not mention it; Assyrian annals tell only of victory. But each soldier remembers the sting of sand and the long day without sight.",
  "assyrians:fire": "Nineveh burns in 612 BCE. The palace of Ashurbanipal collapses, and the fire bakes hard the clay tablets of his great library, thousands of them, including the Epic of Gilgamesh. The empire died in that blaze, but its words were preserved by it, waiting in the ruins until Layard and Rassam dug them out in the 1840s and 1850s.",
  "assyrians:thunder": "Imagined: thunder rolls over Assur during a campaign season, and a soldier on the walls asks his officer whether Adad is angry. The palace scholars are already writing to the king: if Adad thunders when the moon is in a certain sign, the king's enemy will be defeated. The Assyrians trusted thunder as long as experts were there to read it.",
  "assyrians:birth": "Imagined: in a house in Nineveh, a mother in labor clutches an amulet of Pazuzu while the midwife recites an incantation against Lamashtu. The father, a soldier, is away on campaign with the king. Will the child be a scribe, a merchant, or another soldier? The family hopes for a son, and the mother simply hopes to live to see it grow.",
  "assyrians:death": "Royal Assyrian funerals followed ancient rites: the king's body was laid in a stone sarcophagus in the old capital of Assur, with offerings of oil, food and precious goods. A text describes King Esarhaddon's mourners and the gifts sent with him. The ghost must be fed through the kispu offerings by descendants, or it would wander, hungry and dangerous.",

  // ── Sumerians ──
  "sumerians:hunt": "Imagined: at dawn in the marshes near Ur, Sumerian fishermen pole their reed boats through the channels, spearing carp and setting nets for ducks. The city's temple of Nanna takes its share of every catch, recorded on clay. Older hunters still tell how Gilgamesh and Enkidu went to the Cedar Forest and killed its guardian, Humbaba, and wonder whether the gods truly forgave them.",
  "sumerians:battle": "Around 2450 BCE Eannatum of Lagash fights the neighboring city of Umma over a fertile strip of land called the Gu-edena. His Stele of the Vultures shows his soldiers marching in a tight phalanx of shields and spears over the bodies of the enemy, while vultures carry off heads. The war over that boundary ditch lasted generations, the first border dispute we can read.",
  "sumerians:gather": "A Sumerian text called the Farmer's Instructions, written about 1700 BCE, is a father's advice to his son: watch the flood, open the canal gates carefully, keep oxen ready, harvest the barley before it falls over. Reapers work in teams of three. Then the threshing floor, the winnowing, and a prayer to Ninkasi, goddess of beer, for there is barley enough to brew.",
  "sumerians:ritual": "Each year at Ur, the moon god Nanna receives offerings of fat sheep, dates and butter at his ziggurat. Enheduanna, daughter of Sargon of Akkad, served as high priestess there around 2300 BCE and wrote hymns to Inanna, the first author known by name. Priests watch the moon's horns and the flight of birds and ask whether the year will be good. The answer is sought in silence.",
  "sumerians:rain": "In Sumer the sky seldom rains; the land waits on the Tigris and Euphrates. In the Lament for Ur, the storm of Enlil sweeps the city and there is no rain for the fields, no grass for the herds. Salt slowly crept into the fields as irrigation went on for centuries. Farmers switched from wheat to barley, waiting for a better time that did not come.",
  "sumerians:flood": "In the Sumerian story of Ziusudra, the gods decide to send a flood. Enki warns the pious king, and for seven days and seven nights the flood sweeps over the land while a great boat is tossed on the waters. Then Utu, the sun god, shines in. Ziusudra bows before him and is granted eternal life. The Sumerian King List even divides history into before and after the flood.",
  "sumerians:wind": "The Lament for Ur, written around 2000 BCE, mourns a great storm, Enlil's storm, which destroyed the city: the Elamites came like a wind, and the dead lay in the streets like molten bronze. The goddess Ningal pleads with the gods to stop it, but the storm does not cease. The poem is the voice of a whole city grieving for itself.",
  "sumerians:fire": "Imagined: a fire sweeps through the reed houses outside the walls of Uruk on a dry summer night. Reed burns like straw, and within an hour the whole quarter is ash. Neighbors bring jars of water from the canal, a priest calls on Gibil, god of fire, to be calm. By morning they are cutting fresh reeds from the marsh to build again, as their ancestors did.",
  "sumerians:thunder": "Iškur, the Sumerian storm god, rides the clouds and roars over the mountains. His thunder can bring the rain that the northern hills need, or hail that ruins the crops. In hymns he is a wild bull. Imagined: a farmer outside Lagash hears him rumble and looks to the east, unsure whether Iškur brings a blessing or a threat.",
  "sumerians:birth": "The goddess Ninhursag, the mother of the land, shapes children in the womb. A Sumerian incantation compares the child in labor to a boat carrying precious cargo, asking the gods to bring it safely to the quay. Many mothers and infants died. Yet the family imagines a scribe who will learn the wedge script, or perhaps another Gilgamesh, two-thirds god.",
  "sumerians:death": "At Ur around 2500 BCE, Queen Puabi was buried with gold leaves in her hair, lyres, carts and dozens of attendants who seem to have died with her, laid out in rows in the Great Death Pit excavated by Leonard Woolley. For ordinary people, burial was simpler: a grave under the floor, a cup and a plate, so they might not go hungry in the dark underworld.",

  // ── Hittites ──
  "hittites:hunt": "Imagined: on the high Anatolian plateau, Hittite huntsmen ride out with hawks and dogs after deer and wild sheep. On a relief at Alaca Höyük, a hunter faces a stag and a lion attacks a bull. A stag was sacred to the tutelary god of the countryside, and the hunter leaves part of each kill at a rock shrine in thanks.",
  "hittites:battle": "Kadesh, 1274 BCE, from the Hittite side. King Muwatalli II lures Ramesses II with false spies, then sends some 2,500 chariots across the river into the Egyptian camp. The trap nearly works. Sixteen years later Hattusili III and Ramesses sign the first known peace treaty, a copy of which hangs today at the United Nations. Both sides claimed glory, but peace held.",
  "hittites:gather": "In the uplands around Hattusa, farmers harvest emmer and barley before the short summer ends. Vineyards ripen on the slopes, and great grain silos at Hattusa could store food for thousands. The Hittite laws list prices for an ox, a sheep, a measure of wine. A harvest festival honors Telipinu, the god who once vanished in anger and took growth with him.",
  "hittites:ritual": "Hittite priests asked the gods yes-or-no questions using oracles: the flight of birds, the entrails of sheep, and the KIN oracle, a game of symbols moved by an old woman. King Mursili II, during a plague around 1320 BCE, asked again and again why the gods were angry, and wrote his prayers down: what sin of my father must I atone for? Each answer was recorded.",
  "hittites:rain": "The myth of Telipinu tells of a god who disappeared in a rage. Mist covered the windows, smoke filled the house, grain no longer grew, cattle and people stopped giving birth. The gods searched everywhere until a bee found him asleep in a meadow and stung him awake. A ritual brought him home. The Hittites knew what it meant to wait for the world to come back to life.",
  "hittites:wind": "Imagined: a storm sweeps down from the Pontic mountains over Hattusa, tearing at the roofs of the Great Temple and scattering clay tablets kept in the storerooms. Priests ask the oracles whether the Storm God of Hatti is angry. Old men say it is Teshub fighting the dragon Illuyanka again, just as the festival of Purulli tells each spring.",
  "hittites:fire": "Around 1180 BCE Hattusa was abandoned and burned. Archaeologists found the destroyed great temple and citadel. Was it invasion, drought, or the court leaving first? The capital of an empire that had matched Egypt went dark, and Hittite writing vanished. Thousands of tablets baked in the ruins kept their laws and prayers for archaeologists to find over 3,000 years later.",
  "hittites:thunder": "The Storm God of Hatti, Tarhunna, rides a chariot drawn by bulls named Serri and Hurri. He is the king of the gods, and the king of Hatti is his steward. Thunder over Hattusa means the god is present. In the Purulli festival, priests recite how he killed the serpent Illuyanka with the help of the goddess Inara, ensuring the land would prosper.",
  "hittites:birth": "Hittite birth rituals, recorded on tablets from Kizzuwatna, describe a birthing stool, a midwife who speaks spells, and offerings to the Mother Goddesses who spin the fate of the newborn. The family waits outside the room. Will this child become a scribe at the palace, a soldier, a priestess? The goddesses already know; the family can only hope.",
  "hittites:death": "When a Hittite king died, people said he had become a god. For fourteen days his body was burned, his bones gathered with silver tongs and soaked in oil, wrapped in fine cloth and laid on a chair in a stone house. Offerings were made each day, an image of the king was honored, and the court mourned in a precise order that tablets preserve.",

  // ── Phoenicians ──
  "phoenicians:hunt": "Imagined: on the cedar-covered slopes above Byblos, Phoenician hunters chase deer and wild goats, while on the shore below their cousins hunt a different prey: the murex sea snail. Thousands of shells are crushed to make Tyrian purple, a dye worth more than gold. The workshops stink of rotting shellfish, but kings across the sea will wear the color.",
  "phoenicians:battle": "In 332 BCE Tyre, an island city, refused to open its gates to Alexander the Great. For seven months Alexander's engineers built a causeway through the sea while Tyrians sent fire ships against it and fought from their walls. When the city fell, thousands were killed and tens of thousands enslaved. The causeway remains: Tyre is no longer an island.",
  "phoenicians:gather": "Imagined: in the hills behind Sidon, families harvest olives and grapes, pressing oil and wine to load into amphoras for the ships. The Phoenicians were merchants first, so every harvest becomes trade goods. A father teaches his son to count with the new alphabet, the one that will spread to Greece and beyond: aleph, beth, gimel.",
  "phoenicians:ritual": "Before a ship sails, the captain brings a gift to the temple of Melqart in Tyre, or to Astarte. Herodotus visited the temple and saw two pillars, one of gold and one of emerald glowing at night. Sailors ask: will the voyage to Gadir return safely? Small terracotta figures and lamps left at shrines along the coast record their quiet requests.",
  "phoenicians:rain": "Imagined: in Ugarit, a city on the same coast, the myth of Baal told how the storm god was swallowed by Mot, Death, and for a time no rain fell. Phoenician farmers knew the dry summer was a season of waiting. When the first autumn clouds came over the sea, people said Baal had returned from the underworld.",
  "phoenicians:flood": "Lucian, writing in the 2nd century CE, describes it: when rains swell the Adonis River near Byblos, its waters run red with iron-rich soil from the mountains. People said it was the blood of Adonis, killed by a boar, and women mourned him in the Adonia festival. The flood is both a grief and a renewal: the god returns with the spring.",
  "phoenicians:wind": "Imagined: a Phoenician ship out of Tyre, laden with cedar and purple cloth, is caught in a winter gale off Cyprus. The crew lowers the sail and throws cargo overboard, praying to Baal and Melqart. Shipwrecks like those near Ashkelon and Mazarrón show many did not return. Those who reach port bring offerings and tell the story for years.",
  "phoenicians:fire": "Imagined: in the harbor of Sidon a ship's tar catches fire, and the flames leap from deck to deck along the crowded quay. Sailors cut mooring lines and push burning ships into the open water. The glassmakers and dyers come running. Phoenicians lived by the sea, and losing a fleet meant losing a season's trade.",
  "phoenicians:thunder": "Baal Hadad, the storm god, rides the clouds and hurls lightning. In the Ugaritic poems he builds a palace with a window, and when it is opened his voice, the thunder, shakes the earth and makes the mountains tremble. Phoenician sailors heard thunder as the voice of the god who could save or sink them.",
  "phoenicians:birth": "Imagined: a merchant's wife in Byblos gives birth while her husband is away on a voyage to Carthage or Gadir. The midwife calls on Astarte, and a small amulet of the Egyptian dwarf god Bes hangs over the bed. Will the child be a sailor, a scribe, or a founder of a new city like Elissa, who founded Carthage?",
  "phoenicians:death": "In 1923 archaeologists found the sarcophagus of Ahiram, king of Byblos, around 1000 BCE. Its lid carries one of the earliest Phoenician alphabet inscriptions: a curse on anyone who disturbs the tomb. Mourning women are carved on its side, tearing their hair and beating their breasts. Phoenicians grieved loudly and wanted their dead to rest in peace.",

  // ── Palmyrans ──
  "palmyrans:hunt": "Imagined: Palmyrene archers ride out from the oasis into the Syrian steppe after gazelle and wild ass. They were famous bowmen, later serving Rome from Dacia to Britain. A young hunter brings his first gazelle home through the colonnaded street and offers a portion to Bel at the great temple, as his father did before him.",
  "palmyrans:battle": "In 272 CE Queen Zenobia, who had made Palmyra an empire stretching from Egypt to Anatolia, faced Emperor Aurelian near Antioch and then at Emesa. Her heavy cavalry, the clibanarii, charged and were drawn into exhaustion by the Roman retreat. Zenobia fled toward Persia on a camel but was caught at the Euphrates. Her defiance is still celebrated in Syria.",
  "palmyrans:gather": "The spring of Efqa watered gardens of palms, olives and pomegranates in the middle of the desert. But Palmyra's true harvest was trade: caravans from the Euphrates brought silk, spices and pearls. A tax law of 137 CE, carved in Greek and Aramaic, lists dues on everything from slaves to perfumed oil. Gathering, here, meant counting camels.",
  "palmyrans:ritual": "On the feast of the god Bel, priests at the great temple dedicated in 32 CE hold a banquet. Members of the brotherhood eat together on couches, entering with small clay tokens as tickets. Divination and prayer ask Bel, Yarhibol and Aglibol, gods of the sun and moon, whether the caravans will return safely. The temple stood almost two thousand years, until 2015.",
  "palmyrans:rain": "Palmyra lived on its oasis spring and on rain-fed cisterns in the surrounding hills. Imagined: in a dry year the caravan leaders gather in the agora to decide whether to send camels across the desert when the wells may be dry. They wait for the clouds, and for word from the Euphrates, before risking everything.",
  "palmyrans:wind": "Imagined: a dust storm rises from the desert and engulfs the caravan city. The colonnades vanish in yellow air, camels kneel and lower their heads, and merchants shelter in the caravanserais. The storm can last for days and bury a caravan on the open road. Caravan leaders, honored on statues along the colonnade, earned their fame by bringing travelers through such storms.",
  "palmyrans:fire": "After Zenobia's defeat, Palmyra rebelled again in 273 CE. Aurelian returned, sacked the city and burned parts of it. The temple of the sun was looted for treasure. Palmyra never regained its glory. Its citizens, once lords of the caravan route, watched their wealth reduced to ash, and the city slowly became a small desert garrison.",
  "palmyrans:thunder": "Imagined: thunder rolls over the desert on a rare winter night, and rain fills the cisterns and wadis around the oasis. Palmyrenes thank Baalshamin, the lord of the heavens, whose temple stood near the colonnade. Desert people know a storm can bring both flash floods and a year's water, and they greet it with awe.",
  "palmyrans:birth": "Imagined: a girl is born in a merchant house of Palmyra, and an old aunt says that Zenobia too was born here, the daughter of a local family, and became a queen who challenged Rome. The mother laughs, exhausted. The father thinks of the risks: fever, the desert, wars. Still, the family offers incense to Bel and dreams.",
  "palmyrans:death": "Palmyrenes buried their dead in tall tower tombs and underground hypogea outside the city, each body in a niche sealed with a carved portrait. The faces look out calmly, wearing jewelry and naming themselves in Aramaic: alas, so-and-so, son of so-and-so. Many of these busts survive in museums. Families gathered at banquets in the tombs to remember them.",

  // ── Minoans ──
  "minoans:hunt": "Imagined: in the mountains of Crete, young men hunt the agrimi, the wild goat with long curved horns, using bows and dogs. Its horns were prized for making composite bows. The famous bull-leaping frescoes of Knossos show a different contest: youths somersaulting over a charging bull, a dance with the most dangerous animal on the island.",
  "minoans:battle": "The Minoans left surprisingly few images of war, and their palaces had no great walls; perhaps their fleet was their defense. Imagined: Minoan ships drive off raiders near Crete, the crew rowing hard and loosing arrows. Around 1450 BCE, Mycenaean Greeks took over Knossos, and Minoan Crete gradually became a Greek world.",
  "minoans:gather": "At Knossos, great clay jars called pithoi, taller than a person, stored olive oil, wine and grain. The Harvester Vase from Hagia Triada shows a procession of farmers with tools on their shoulders, singing, led by a man shaking a sistrum. It is one of the most joyful scenes in ancient art: the harvest as a festival.",
  "minoans:ritual": "On peak sanctuaries like Mount Juktas, Minoans lit bonfires and left small clay figures of people and animals, perhaps asking healing or protection. In 1979 archaeologists at Anemospilia found a temple destroyed by an earthquake around 1700 BCE, with what seemed to be a young man laid on an altar. What the priests asked the gods in that final moment is unknown.",
  "minoans:rain": "Imagined: in a dry summer on Crete, farmers climb to a peak sanctuary to leave clay figurines and light fires, asking the goddess for rain. The olive trees endure, but grain withers. They look south to the sea and north to the clouds over Thera, waiting. A century later, the change they did not imagine would come from that direction.",
  "minoans:flood": "Around 1600 BCE the volcano of Thera, Santorini, erupted in one of the largest eruptions of human history. The town of Akrotiri was buried, though its people seem to have fled in time. Tsunamis may have struck the north coast of Crete, and the ash spread across the Aegean. Some see in this the seed of Plato's story of Atlantis.",
  "minoans:wind": "Imagined: a great storm drives across the Aegean, and Minoan ships returning from Egypt with ivory and gold race for the harbor of Kommos. The sails tear and rowers strain against the waves. The Minoans were masters of the sea, and their frescoes of ships at Akrotiri show them proud, but every family knew the sea could take its sons.",
  "minoans:fire": "Around 1450 BCE fire swept through the palaces of Crete. At Knossos the flames baked the clay tablets written in Linear B, the script of the Mycenaean Greeks who then ruled there, preserving their inventories of sheep, oil and slaves. The palaces at Phaistos and Zakros burned too. Whether by war or disaster, an era ended in flames.",
  "minoans:thunder": "Imagined: thunder rolls over Mount Ida, and Cretans say the young god has been born again in the cave. Later Greeks believed Zeus was hidden as an infant in a Cretan cave, while warriors called Kouretes clashed their shields so his father would not hear him cry. Perhaps the Minoans already heard a god in the storm.",
  "minoans:birth": "Imagined: a mother in a Cretan villa gives birth with the help of women of her family, while a clay figurine of a goddess holding snakes watches over the house. Minoan art shows women as priestesses and leaders. The family wonders whether this girl might serve at the palace sanctuary, or leap bulls in the court at Knossos.",
  "minoans:death": "At Hagia Triada a painted stone sarcophagus from about 1400 BCE shows a funeral: women pour offerings into a vessel between double axes, a man plays a lyre, a bull lies sacrificed on a table, and the dead man stands before his tomb receiving gifts of a boat and animals. It is one of the clearest glimpses of how Minoans honored their dead.",

  // ── Greeks ──
  "greeks:hunt": "In the Odyssey, young Odysseus hunts a great boar on Mount Parnassus with his grandfather's sons. The boar charges and gashes his leg above the knee before he kills it. Decades later, the scar lets his old nurse Eurycleia recognize him when he returns home in disguise. For Greeks, the hunt tested a young man and left marks that told his story.",
  "greeks:battle": "Marathon, 490 BCE. About ten thousand Athenians and Plataeans face the Persian army on the plain. The general Miltiades thins his center and strengthens his wings; the hoplites run the last stretch under arrow fire, and the wings close around the Persians. 192 Athenians die and are buried in the mound that still stands there. Aeschylus, who fought there, was proudest of that day.",
  "greeks:gather": "Hesiod's Works and Days, written around 700 BCE, is a farmer's calendar: when the Pleiades rise, begin the harvest; when they set, plow. He warns his lazy brother Perses that the gods hid livelihood from humans, and only work brings it. In autumn the olive harvest, in late summer the grapes trodden in the vat, and a cup of wine in the shade.",
  "greeks:ritual": "At Delphi, the Pythia sits on a tripod in the temple of Apollo and speaks the god's answer. In 480 BCE the Athenians, terrified of the Persian invasion, were told that a wooden wall would save them. Themistocles argued that the wall meant their ships. They took to the sea and won at Salamis. People came to Delphi for advice on wars, colonies and marriages.",
  "greeks:rain": "In dry years Greek farmers prayed to Zeus for rain. Marcus Aurelius preserved the Athenian prayer: rain, rain, dear Zeus, on the fields of the Athenians. Farmers watched for the clouds to gather over Mount Hymettus, a sign that the rains would come. The whole agricultural year waited on the autumn rains, which fell just in time or not at all.",
  "greeks:flood": "Deucalion and Pyrrha, the Greek flood story: Zeus, disgusted by human wickedness, floods the world. Deucalion, warned by his father Prometheus, builds a chest and floats for nine days until he lands on Mount Parnassus. Told to throw the bones of their mother behind them, they throw stones, which become new people. Greeks pointed to the place on Parnassus where they landed.",
  "greeks:wind": "In the Odyssey, the wind god Aeolus gives Odysseus a bag holding all the storm winds, leaving only the gentle west wind to carry him home. Within sight of Ithaca, his crew opens the bag, thinking it holds gold. The winds burst out and blow them back across the sea. A storm, for Greeks, could come from the gods, or from one's own foolishness.",
  "greeks:fire": "In 356 BCE a man named Herostratus burned the Temple of Artemis at Ephesus, one of the Seven Wonders, simply to make his name famous. The Ephesians condemned him and forbade anyone to speak his name, but historians recorded it anyway. Legend says the fire burned the night Alexander the Great was born, while Artemis was away attending the birth.",
  "greeks:thunder": "Zeus the thunderer, whose lightning bolts were forged by the Cyclopes. A place struck by lightning became sacred, fenced off and left untouched. When thunder rolled over Olympia, Greeks felt the god was present, and great festivals there honored him. In the Iliad, Zeus thunders from Mount Ida to signal which side he favors. The sound was a message.",
  "greeks:birth": "A Greek birth was attended by a midwife and the women of the household, with prayers to Artemis and Eileithyia. On the fifth or seventh day the child was carried around the hearth in the Amphidromia, and on the tenth day named. Euripides' Medea says she would rather stand three times in battle than bear one child. Yet who knew whether this boy might become a Pericles, or a Socrates.",
  "greeks:death": "The body is washed, dressed and laid out at home for the prothesis, women lamenting around it and tearing their hair. Then before dawn the ekphora: the procession to the cemetery, with flutes and mourners. A coin for Charon is placed in the mouth to pay the ferry across the Styx. Painted vases show the scene. Tombs were visited with oil and ribbons for generations.",

  // ── Athenians ──
  "athenians:hunt": "Xenophon, an Athenian who fought with the Ten Thousand, wrote the Cynegeticus, a book on hunting hares with hounds. He describes how the dogs pick up the scent at dawn, the nets spread across the paths, the hunters running through the hills of Attica. For him the hunt trained young men for war and for virtue: patience, endurance, and quick judgment.",
  "athenians:battle": "Salamis, September 480 BCE. The Persians have burned the Acropolis, and the Athenians watch from their ships as smoke rises over their city. Themistocles lures the Persian fleet into the narrow strait, where its numbers become a weakness. Greek triremes ram and shear oars. Aeschylus, who fought there, later wrote The Persians, imagining the defeat through the eyes of the enemy.",
  "athenians:gather": "The olive was Athena's gift to Athens, given when she won the contest with Poseidon for the city. Sacred olive trees, the moriai, stood in the countryside, and their oil filled the prize amphoras of the Panathenaic Games. Harvest was hard work, beating the branches with sticks. Cutting down a sacred olive could bring exile or death.",
  "athenians:ritual": "Every autumn, thousands walked the Sacred Way from Athens to Eleusis for the Mysteries of Demeter and Persephone. Initiates were sworn to secrecy, and so faithfully kept it that we still do not know what they saw. Cicero, an initiate, said they learned to live with joy and to die with better hope. A quiet awe surrounded the whole procession.",
  "athenians:rain": "In 430 BCE, during the war with Sparta, Athens was crowded with refugees from the countryside, and a plague broke out. Thucydides, who caught it and survived, described the fever, the thirst, the bodies piled in temples. Pericles himself died. The Athenians waited for the war, the plague, and their fortunes to change. Their world never quite returned.",
  "athenians:flood": "Imagined: a winter storm sends the Kephisos and Ilissos rivers over their banks, flooding the low fields of Attica and the edges of the city. Farmers lose their seed, and potters in the Kerameikos haul their wares to higher ground. Athenians recalled that Poseidon, angry at losing the city to Athena, had once flooded the Thriasian plain.",
  "athenians:wind": "On the Roman-era Tower of the Winds in the Athenian agora, eight winds are carved as flying figures: Boreas, the cold north wind, blowing a conch; Zephyros, scattering flowers. Athenians honored Boreas after a storm wrecked part of the Persian fleet in 480 BCE, believing the north wind, their in-law through Oreithyia, had answered their prayers.",
  "athenians:fire": "In 480 BCE the Persians under Xerxes captured Athens and burned the temples on the Acropolis. The Athenians, watching from Salamis, swore not to rebuild them, as a memorial. Thirty years later Pericles broke that oath and raised the Parthenon. Archaeologists later found the broken statues the Athenians had buried after the fire, the Persian debris.",
  "athenians:thunder": "In Aristophanes' comedy The Clouds, Socrates tells a farmer that thunder is not Zeus but clouds colliding and bursting. The audience laughed, but the play reflected a real anxiety: philosophers questioning the gods. In 399 BCE Socrates was tried for impiety and corrupting the youth, and he drank the hemlock. Thunder was a question of faith in Athens.",
  "athenians:birth": "On the tenth day after birth, Athenian families held a feast to name the child, often after a grandfather. An olive wreath on the door meant a boy, a tuft of wool a girl. Pericles was born around 495 BCE; his mother Agariste dreamed she gave birth to a lion. No one knew whether a newborn would lead Athens or die of a fever before spring.",
  "athenians:death": "Every winter during the Peloponnesian War, Athens held a public funeral for those killed in battle. The bones were laid in cypress coffins, one for each tribe, with an empty bier for the missing. In 431 BCE Pericles gave the Funeral Oration recorded by Thucydides: the whole earth is the tomb of famous men. Mourning became a statement of what the city was.",

  // ── Spartans ──
  "spartans:hunt": "Spartan boys in the agoge were underfed on purpose and encouraged to steal food; being caught meant a beating. Plutarch tells of a boy who hid a stolen fox under his cloak and let it gnaw his belly rather than reveal it, dying in silence. Hunting with the famous Laconian hounds trained them for war and fed the common messes.",
  "spartans:battle": "Thermopylae, August 480 BCE. King Leonidas and three hundred Spartans, with several thousand allies, hold the narrow pass against Xerxes. For two days the Persian waves break on the Greek shields. Then a local man, Ephialtes, shows the Persians a mountain path. Leonidas sends most allies away and fights to the last. The epitaph: go tell the Spartans, passerby, that here, obedient to their laws, we lie.",
  "spartans:gather": "Spartan citizens did not farm; the helots did. These enslaved Messenians and Laconians worked the fields and handed over a fixed share of barley, wine and oil to their Spartan masters. Each year the ephors formally declared war on the helots, a sign of how afraid Spartans were of them. The harvest fed the soldiers but rested on fear.",
  "spartans:ritual": "Spartans were famous for their piety: before every battle the king sacrificed and read the omens, and they would not march during sacred festivals. At Plataea in 479 BCE, the omens were bad and the Spartans stood under arrow fire, waiting, until the king Pausanias prayed to Hera and the signs turned favorable. Only then did they charge.",
  "spartans:rain": "In 490 BCE Athens begged Sparta for help against the Persians at Marathon. The Spartans agreed, but said they could not march until the full moon, during the Carneia festival. They arrived the day after the battle, inspected the Persian dead and praised the Athenians. Spartan waiting was a matter of religion, even when the world could not wait.",
  "spartans:flood": "Imagined: the Eurotas river, swollen by winter rains from Mount Taygetus, floods its banks below Sparta, where boys of the agoge have their reed beds. The boys pull reeds from the water with their bare hands, as they always did, without knives. For Spartans even the river's flood was another test of endurance.",
  "spartans:wind": "Imagined: on the slopes of Taygetus a winter storm tears through the training grounds, and Spartan boys, given one cloak a year and no shoes, huddle together in silence. Their instructors watch. Complaining would shame them. Plutarch says Spartan boys learned to endure cold and hunger so that nothing in war would surprise them.",
  "spartans:fire": "Imagined: lightning strikes a temple on the acropolis of Sparta, and the citizens rush to fight the fire while priests consult the omens. Sparta had no walls, proud that its soldiers were its walls, but fire was an enemy that could not be fought with spears. The elders ask the oracle at Delphi which god is angry.",
  "spartans:thunder": "In 464 BCE a great earthquake struck Sparta, destroying much of the city and, it was said, killing many of its young men. The helots revolted. The Spartans, struggling, asked Athens for help, then sent the Athenian troops home in suspicion, beginning the rift that would lead to the Peloponnesian War. The earth roared like thunder.",
  "spartans:birth": "According to Plutarch, a newborn Spartan was brought before the elders, who inspected it. If it was healthy, it would be raised; if weak, it was said to be left at the Apothetae, a place near Mount Taygetus. Modern historians doubt the extent of this. Mothers, it was said, bathed newborns in wine to test their strength. Hope and fear came together at the cradle.",
  "spartans:death": "Spartan law, Plutarch says, allowed the name of the dead on a tombstone only for men who died in war and women who died in childbirth. Mourning was short, eleven days, and restrained. When a king died, however, the whole land mourned loudly, with helots forced to wail. A mother told her son: come back with your shield, or on it.",

  // ── Macedonians ──
  "macedonians:hunt": "At Vergina, the tomb believed to be Philip II's has a painted frieze of a royal hunt: horsemen and men on foot with spears chase a lion, a boar and a bear through the forest, and a young man, perhaps Alexander, rides at the center. In Macedonia a man could not recline at the banquet until he had killed a boar without a net.",
  "macedonians:battle": "Gaugamela, October 331 BCE. Alexander faces Darius III and a vast Persian army on a plain chosen for chariots. He leads his Companion cavalry to the right, opening a gap in the Persian line, and drives straight at Darius, who flees. The Persian Empire falls. Alexander was twenty-five, and his soldiers would follow him as far as India.",
  "macedonians:gather": "Imagined: in the green valleys of Macedonia, below Mount Olympus, farmers harvest wheat and gather grapes while timber cutters fell pine and fir for ships, Macedonia's greatest export. Many sons of these families are away in Asia with Alexander. The harvest is brought in by old men, women and children, who send letters asking when they will come home.",
  "macedonians:ritual": "In 332 BCE Alexander made a dangerous journey through the Libyan desert to the oracle of Ammon at Siwa. The chief priest greeted him, by some accounts, as son of Zeus-Ammon. Alexander never revealed what he asked; he wrote to his mother Olympias that he would tell her in person. He died before he could.",
  "macedonians:rain": "In 326 BCE, on the banks of the Hyphasis river in India, the Macedonian army, after eight years of marching, refused to go further. The monsoon rains had fallen for seventy days, armor rusted, and the men longed for home. Alexander sulked in his tent for three days, waiting for them to change their minds. They did not. He turned back.",
  "macedonians:flood": "Imagined: the Axios river floods the plain near Pella after heavy rains, and the royal capital's harbor lake overflows. Fields of wheat lie underwater. Macedonians were used to river valleys; this was a hard year, but the young king Philip will soon build drainage works and fortifications, transforming his poor kingdom into the strongest in Greece.",
  "macedonians:wind": "In 325 BCE Alexander led part of his army back from India through the Gedrosian Desert. Arrian tells of a flash flood from a sudden storm that swept away the camp, wives and children, and of burning winds and sandstorms. Thousands died of thirst and heat. It was the cruelest march of the campaign, and Alexander shared every hardship.",
  "macedonians:fire": "In 330 BCE, at Persepolis, the ceremonial capital of Persia, Alexander's men burned the palace of Xerxes after a drunken feast. Some say it was revenge for the burning of Athens; the Athenian courtesan Thais urged it on. Plutarch says Alexander soon regretted it. The charred ruins still stand in Iran.",
  "macedonians:thunder": "Plutarch says that on the night before her wedding to Philip, Olympias dreamed a thunderbolt struck her womb and a great fire spread from it. Philip later dreamed he sealed her womb with a lion's seal. Seers said she was pregnant with a son as brave as a lion. Alexander grew up believing he might be the son of Zeus.",
  "macedonians:birth": "Alexander was born in Pella in July 356 BCE. On the same day, Plutarch says, Philip received three pieces of news: his general Parmenion had beaten the Illyrians, his horse had won at Olympia, and his son was born. The seers said a son born amid three victories would be invincible. No one guessed how far he would go, or how young he would die.",
  "macedonians:death": "When Alexander died in Babylon in June 323 BCE, aged thirty-two, his generals quarreled over the body. A magnificent funeral cart took two years to build. Ptolemy hijacked it and brought it to Egypt; the tomb in Alexandria was visited by Caesar and Augustus, but its location is lost. A year earlier, grieving his friend Hephaestion, Alexander himself had ordered the manes of horses shorn in mourning.",

  // ── Romans ──
  "romans:hunt": "In 80 CE Emperor Titus opened the Flavian Amphitheatre, the Colosseum, with a hundred days of games; some 9,000 animals were killed in the venationes. Lions, bears, elephants and ostriches were hunted in front of fifty thousand spectators. Animals were captured across the empire, from Mesopotamia to Mauretania. Some Romans, like Cicero, found the slaughter tiresome; most of the crowd roared.",
  "romans:battle": "Cannae, 2 August 216 BCE. Hannibal lets his center bend back while his African infantry and cavalry close around the Roman legions from both sides. Some fifty thousand Romans die in a single afternoon, among them a consul and eighty senators. Rome does not surrender. The Senate forbids public mourning beyond thirty days, raises new legions, and fights on for fourteen years.",
  "romans:gather": "Cato the Elder's De Agri Cultura, written around 160 BCE, gives instructions for running a farm: how to press olives, how to make wine, what to feed slaves when they are sick (less). At Rome, grain from Egypt and Sicily arrived by ship at Ostia, and the annona fed the city's poor. Harvest festivals honored Ceres, and the Saturnalia ended the year in feasting.",
  "romans:ritual": "Before any public act, Roman augurs watched the sky: the flight of birds, the behavior of the sacred chickens. In 249 BCE the consul Publius Claudius Pulcher, before the naval battle of Drepana, was told the chickens would not eat. Furious, he threw them into the sea: let them drink, then. He lost his fleet. Romans remembered the lesson for centuries.",
  "romans:rain": "On 24 August 79 CE, the sky darkened over Pompeii as Vesuvius erupted. Pliny the Younger, watching from Misenum across the bay, described a cloud shaped like an umbrella pine and the people waiting, unsure whether to flee or stay. His uncle Pliny the Elder sailed to rescue friends and died on the beach at Stabiae. The ash fell like snow, and the world did not wait.",
  "romans:flood": "The Tiber flooded Rome again and again. In 15 CE, Tacitus says, the river swelled and filled the low parts of the city, collapsing buildings and drowning people. The Senate debated diverting its tributaries, but towns upstream protested that the gods had set the rivers where they were. Romans learned to live with the Tiber's moods; marks on churches still show flood levels.",
  "romans:wind": "In 255 BCE, after a victory against Carthage, a Roman fleet of over 300 ships was caught in a storm off Camarina in Sicily. The consuls, ignoring the pilots' warnings, hugged the coast, and most of the fleet was smashed on the rocks. Tens of thousands drowned. It was one of the worst maritime disasters in antiquity; the Romans built a new fleet.",
  "romans:fire": "On the night of 18 July 64 CE a fire broke out near the Circus Maximus and burned for six days. Of Rome's fourteen districts, three were destroyed and seven badly damaged. Nero, away at Antium, returned and opened his gardens to the homeless. Rumors said he sang of the fall of Troy as the city burned. Tacitus says he blamed the Christians.",
  "romans:thunder": "Romans read lightning carefully: a place struck by a thunderbolt was sacred, enclosed and called a bidental. Priests interpreted the direction and the timing. In 44 BCE omens before Caesar's assassination included storms and strange lights in the sky. His wife Calpurnia dreamed of disaster. Caesar went to the Senate anyway, and on the Ides of March he died.",
  "romans:birth": "A newborn Roman was laid on the ground before the father, who could lift it up to accept it into the family. On the ninth day for boys, the eighth for girls, the dies lustricus, the child was purified, named, and given a bulla amulet to wear. Gaius Octavius, born in 63 BCE in a small house on the Palatine, would become Augustus. No one could foresee that.",
  "romans:death": "When a Roman noble died, the funeral procession wound to the Forum. Actors wore the wax masks of his ancestors, imagines, so that the dead consuls and generals walked again with him. His son gave a eulogy from the rostra. Polybius found it the most inspiring sight a young man could see. Then the body was burned outside the city walls.",

  // ── Carthaginians ──
  "carthaginians:hunt": "Carthage captured wild elephants in the forests of North Africa and trained them for war. Imagined: hunters in the Atlas foothills drive a herd into a stockade, choose young animals, and walk them slowly toward the city's great stables in the walls, which held three hundred elephants. A captured elephant was a soldier worth a hundred men.",
  "carthaginians:battle": "In 218 BCE Hannibal crosses the Alps with some 40,000 men and 37 elephants, losing many to snow, cliffs and mountain tribes. Polybius says he showed his starving soldiers the plains of Italy from the pass to give them hope. Then came victories at the Trebia, Lake Trasimene and Cannae. Hannibal spent fifteen years in Italy and never took Rome.",
  "carthaginians:gather": "The Carthaginian Mago wrote a famous treatise on agriculture in 28 books. After Carthage fell in 146 BCE, the Roman Senate ordered it translated into Latin, the only Carthaginian books spared. Carthaginians farmed olives, grapes, figs and pomegranates on rich estates in Tunisia. Cato showed a fresh Carthaginian fig in the Senate to warn how close and prosperous the enemy was.",
  "carthaginians:ritual": "Carthaginians worshiped Baal Hammon and Tanit, and before great undertakings consulted the gods with offerings. At the sanctuary archaeologists call the tophet, thousands of small urns of burned infant bones were buried with stelae dedicated to Tanit. Scholars still debate whether they were child sacrifices, as Greek and Roman writers claimed, or a cemetery for infants who died young.",
  "carthaginians:rain": "After the First Punic War in 241 BCE, Carthage had to pay a huge indemnity to Rome, and its unpaid mercenaries revolted. For three years the city waited, besieged by its own former soldiers. Polybius called it the most savage war he knew. Carthaginians endured, waiting for Hamilcar Barca to crush the revolt, which at last he did.",
  "carthaginians:flood": "Imagined: a winter storm sends the Bagradas river over its banks, flooding the farms on the plain below Carthage. Farmers move their families to the hills and wait for the waters to fall. Later the river silted up and changed its course, leaving Utica, once a port, far from the sea. Carthaginians knew rivers rearranged the land.",
  "carthaginians:wind": "Imagined: a Carthaginian trading ship rounds the Pillars of Heracles, heading south along the coast of Africa as Hanno the Navigator did around 500 BCE. A storm from the Atlantic strikes. The crew prays to Tanit and Baal, while the steersman keeps her before the wind. The Periplus of Hanno told of fires on the shores and hairy people the explorers called gorillas.",
  "carthaginians:fire": "Spring 146 BCE. After a three-year siege, Scipio Aemilianus storms Carthage. Fighting rages for six days through the streets around the Byrsa hill, the houses set ablaze. The surviving 50,000 Carthaginians are enslaved. Polybius, standing beside Scipio, saw him weep as the city burned, quoting Homer: a day will come when sacred Troy shall perish. He was thinking of Rome.",
  "carthaginians:thunder": "Imagined: thunder rolls over the Gulf of Tunis as the Carthaginian fleet sits in the circular military harbor, the cothon, where some 220 warships could be housed. From the admiral's island tower, lookouts watch the sky. Thunder over the sea means the gods are speaking; a priest at the temple of Baal Hammon is asked what it means for the war.",
  "carthaginians:birth": "Hannibal was born around 247 BCE, son of Hamilcar Barca. Livy says that at age nine, before going to Spain with his father, he was made to swear at an altar never to be a friend of Rome. Imagined: in a house in Carthage a new child is born, and the family wonders if it will grow up to carry such an oath, or live in peace.",
  "carthaginians:death": "Carthaginians buried their dead in rock-cut tombs and shafts on the hills around the city, with lamps, amulets, razors and small masks to frighten demons. In the necropolis archaeologists found masks with grimacing faces and stelae of priests and priestesses. A 2016 study of a young man's skeleton from Byrsa hill found European ancestry, a reminder of the city's wide world.",

  // ── Aztecs ──
  "aztecs:hunt": "During the month of Quecholli, the Mexica held a great ceremonial hunt on the Hill of Zacatepec in honor of Mixcoatl, the Cloud Serpent, god of the hunt. Hunters made arrows for days, then formed a long line and drove deer, rabbits and coyotes across the slopes. Sahagún's informants describe the hunters returning with the game and the honors given to those who caught the most.",
  "aztecs:battle": "On the night of 30 June 1520, the Spaniards and their Tlaxcalan allies tried to slip out of Tenochtitlan along the Tlacopan causeway. A woman fetching water saw them and raised the alarm. Mexica warriors in canoes attacked from both sides; hundreds of Spaniards and thousands of allies drowned or died, weighed down by gold. The Spaniards called it the Noche Triste. The Mexica called it a victory.",
  "aztecs:gather": "On the chinampas, the floating gardens of Lake Texcoco, families harvest maize, beans, squash, chili and amaranth. The great market at Tlatelolco, which amazed Bernal Díaz, was full of produce, turkeys, cacao and obsidian. During the harvest festivals of Huey Tozoztli, young women carried maize ears to the temple of Chicomecoatl, the maize goddess, to bless the seed for the next year.",
  "aztecs:ritual": "Every 52 years, when the two calendars returned to the same day, the Mexica feared the world might end. All fires were extinguished. On the night of the New Fire, priests climbed the Hill of the Star and watched for the Pleiades to cross the zenith. When they did, a fire was kindled on a captive's chest and carried by runners to every hearth. The last was held in 1507.",
  "aztecs:rain": "The Mexica waited anxiously each spring for Tlaloc, the rain god, to send the rains. The drought and famine of 1454, One Rabbit, was remembered for generations: people sold themselves into slavery to the Totonacs for maize. In the festivals of Atlcahualo, offerings to the tlaloque, the rain spirits, were made on the mountains. When the rains came, the whole valley exhaled.",
  "aztecs:flood": "Around 1500 the ruler Ahuitzotl built an aqueduct from the springs of Coyoacán to Tenochtitlan, ignoring a warning. The water came so strong that it flooded the city. Houses collapsed, chinampas were drowned, and Ahuitzotl himself was said to have hit his head escaping. Nezahualcoyotl of Texcoco had earlier built a great dike to protect the city from the lake.",
  "aztecs:wind": "Ehecatl, the wind god, an aspect of Quetzalcoatl, sweeps the road for the rain gods with his breath. His round temples let the wind circle without corners. In the Legend of the Suns, the second world, the Sun of Wind, ended when hurricanes destroyed everything and its people became monkeys. A great storm reminded the Mexica that worlds could end.",
  "aztecs:fire": "In the years before the Spanish arrived, the Mexica recorded strange omens. Among them, the temple of Huitzilopochtli caught fire by itself; the more water the people threw on it, the more it burned. A comet appeared in daylight. A woman was heard crying at night: my children, we must go far away. Sahagún's informants remembered these as signs of the end.",
  "aztecs:thunder": "Tlaloc rules from Tlalocan, the paradise of the rain gods. Thunder is the sound of his helpers, the tlaloque, striking their water jars. People killed by lightning or drowning were not cremated but buried, and went to Tlalocan, a place of eternal spring. A thunderstorm over the lake was frightening, but also a sign of the rain that fed the maize.",
  "aztecs:birth": "When a Mexica child was born, the midwife shouted war cries: the mother had fought a battle and captured a prisoner. A mother who died in childbirth was honored as a warrior, the mocihuaquetzqueh. The midwife told a baby boy that his true home was the battlefield. The child's name depended on the day sign in the tonalpohualli calendar; diviners read whether its fate would be good or harsh.",
  "aztecs:death": "The Mexica believed the destination of the dead depended on how they died, not how they lived. Most went to Mictlan, a four-year journey through nine levels, guided by a small dog. The body was wrapped and burned with offerings, and the ashes kept. For the next four years families offered food on the anniversaries. Warriors who fell in battle accompanied the sun.",

  // ── Mayans ──
  "mayans:hunt": "In the Popol Vuh, the hero twins Hunahpu and Xbalanque are expert blowgun hunters. They shoot the arrogant bird-demon Seven Macaw from his tree, knocking out his teeth. Maya hunters in the forests of Petén went after deer, peccary and turkey with spears and blowguns, asking permission from the lords of the animals before entering the forest.",
  "mayans:battle": "In 562 CE the city of Caracol, allied with powerful Calakmul, defeated mighty Tikal in a star war, a battle timed by the planet Venus. Tikal's king Wak Chan K'awiil was probably captured and sacrificed, and for over a century no monuments were raised there. Then in 695 Tikal's king Jasaw Chan K'awiil defeated Calakmul. Maya wars were long rivalries between great families.",
  "mayans:gather": "In the Popol Vuh, the gods tried to make humans from mud and wood, failing each time, until they ground yellow and white maize into dough and shaped the first true people. Maya farmers still plant milpas of maize, beans and squash. At harvest, families offer the first ears to the earth and to the ancestors. For the Maya, maize is flesh, not just food.",
  "mayans:ritual": "In Maya kingdoms, the king himself drew his own blood to speak with the ancestors. On Yaxchilan Lintel 24, from 709 CE, Lady K'ab'al Xook pulls a thorned rope through her tongue while King Itzamnaaj Bahlam holds a torch above her. In the next lintel, a vision serpent rises from the smoke of blood-stained paper and an ancestor emerges from its mouth to speak.",
  "mayans:rain": "Between about 800 and 1000 CE, the great Maya cities of the southern lowlands were abandoned one by one. Lake sediments show a series of severe droughts. Kings who had claimed to bring rain through Chaak could no longer do so. Imagined: a farmer at Copán waits in the dry season, watching clouds that do not break, and decides to move north with his family.",
  "mayans:flood": "In the Popol Vuh, the gods destroy the wooden people, who had no hearts or minds, with a great flood of black rain. Their animals and pots turn on them, their dogs speak, and those who survived became monkeys. Then the gods tried again, making humans from maize. The flood was a cleansing, a step toward a better creation.",
  "mayans:wind": "The Maya word hurakan, from the K'iche' god Huracán, the one-legged creator and storm, gave us the word hurricane. In the Popol Vuh he is the Heart of Sky, who sent the flood. Each autumn hurricanes from the Caribbean sweep the Yucatán. Imagined: a village near Tulum shelters in stone buildings while Huracán passes, and fishermen pull their canoes far from the shore.",
  "mayans:fire": "Imagined: in the dry season, a Maya farmer burns his cleared plot before planting, as Maya farmers do still. The smoke rises over the forest, and a shaman, an aj q'ij, makes an offering to the earth before the burn. Fire was part of the cycle: from the ashes, the maize will grow. In 1562 the friar Diego de Landa burned Maya books at Maní, and the Maya wept.",
  "mayans:thunder": "Chaak, the rain god, strikes the clouds with his axe of lightning to release the rain. His long-nosed masks cover the buildings of Uxmal and Kabah. Even today, in Yucatán, farmers hold the Ch'a Chaak ceremony, asking for rain with an offering of maize drink, while boys tied to the altar legs croak like frogs. Thunder is Chaak coming to the fields.",
  "mayans:birth": "In Palenque, the inscriptions of the Temple of the Cross tell how K'inich Janaab' Pakal was born in 603 CE. He took the throne at twelve and ruled for 68 years. Imagined: a Maya family brings a newborn to a day-keeper, who reads its birth day in the 260-day calendar. Will the child be a ruler, a scribe, or a farmer? The calendar offers clues.",
  "mayans:death": "In 1952 Alberto Ruz opened a hidden staircase in the Temple of the Inscriptions at Palenque and found the tomb of K'inich Janaab' Pakal, who died in 683 CE. He lay in a great sarcophagus wearing a jade mask, its lid carved with him falling into, or rising from, the jaws of the underworld. Ordinary Maya were buried under their house floors, close to the family.",

  // ── Incas ──
  "incas:hunt": "The Inca held a great royal hunt called the chaku. Thousands of men formed a human circle many kilometers wide and slowly closed it, driving vicuñas, guanacos and deer into the middle. Vicuñas were sheared and released; their fine wool belonged to the Inca. Predators were killed and some deer taken for meat. The chaku is still practiced in Peru today.",
  "incas:battle": "On 16 November 1532 at Cajamarca, the Sapa Inca Atahualpa, fresh from victory over his brother Huáscar, entered the plaza carried on a litter with thousands of unarmed attendants. Friar Valverde handed him a breviary, which he threw to the ground. Pizarro's 168 men opened fire with guns and cannon, charged with horses, and seized Atahualpa. Thousands of Andeans were killed in a single afternoon.",
  "incas:gather": "In the Inca empire, the harvest belonged to three: the gods, the Inca, and the community. Terraces climbed the mountainsides, and freeze-dried potatoes, chuño, were stored in thousands of qollqa storehouses along the roads. In May, the festival of Aymoray celebrated the maize harvest, with songs and the corn carried home. The Inca himself plowed the first furrow in the sacred field.",
  "incas:ritual": "Capacocha: in a time of crisis or for a new ruler, chosen children from across the empire were brought to Cusco, then led to the high peaks and offered to the mountain gods. In 1999 three were found on Llullaillaco, near 6,700 meters, so well preserved they seemed asleep. Their families were honored. To the Inca it was the highest gift, binding the empire to the apus.",
  "incas:rain": "In the Andes, the Inca built astronomical towers and watched the Pleiades, the Collca, each June. If they shone bright, rains would be good; if dim, it would be dry. Modern researchers found this works: El Niño years bring high thin clouds that dim the stars. Andean farmers still watch the Pleiades in June to decide when to plant potatoes.",
  "incas:flood": "Imagined: an El Niño year brings torrential rains to the coast, and the rivers below the Andes overflow, sweeping away fields and roads. The Inca's officials open the storehouses to feed the people. Andean myth tells of an ancient flood, the unu pachakuti, sent by Viracocha to destroy the first giant people before he made humans anew at Lake Titicaca.",
  "incas:wind": "Imagined: a winter storm sweeps the high puna, and a chasqui runner on the royal road fights the wind and sleet to reach the next post, a quipu of knotted cords in his bag. The relay could carry a message 240 kilometers a day. In the tambo he passes the message and collapses by the fire. The road must not stop.",
  "incas:fire": "In 1536 Manco Inca, who had been crowned by the Spaniards as a puppet, rebelled and besieged Cusco with a huge army. His warriors hurled red-hot stones wrapped in burning cotton onto the thatched roofs, and the city burned. The Spaniards, trapped in two buildings, made a desperate charge on the fortress of Sacsayhuamán. The siege failed, and Manco retreated to Vilcabamba.",
  "incas:thunder": "Illapa, the thunder god, carries a sling and a club. Thunder is the crack of his sling, lightning the flash of his clothes, and rain the water he pours from a jug held by his sister in the Milky Way. People struck by lightning and survived were believed chosen to become healers. Twins, too, were considered children of Illapa.",
  "incas:birth": "Inca children were not named until weaned, around age two, at the rutuchikuy, the first haircut, when relatives cut locks of hair and gave gifts. The child's hair and nails were kept. Pachacuti, born around 1418, transformed a small kingdom into the largest empire in the Americas. Imagined: a mother in a village near Cusco hopes her son will survive to that day.",
  "incas:death": "The Inca kings did not truly die. Their mummies, mallquis, stayed in their palaces, attended by servants, given food and drink, consulted on decisions and carried to festivals. Each royal lineage, panaca, cared for its ancestor. The Spanish were astonished. Commoners too were placed in caves or chullpa towers, and families visited them with chicha and food on feast days.",

  // ── Haudenosaunee ──
  "haudenosaunee:hunt": "In autumn, after the harvest, Haudenosaunee men left the longhouse villages for the hunting grounds, often for months, tracking white-tailed deer through the forests of what is now New York. Hunters gave thanks to the animal's spirit and shared the meat. The Thanksgiving Address, spoken at the opening of gatherings, gives thanks to the animals who still offer themselves as food.",
  "haudenosaunee:battle": "In 1779 George Washington ordered the Sullivan Expedition against the Haudenosaunee nations allied with Britain. American troops burned over forty towns, orchards and fields of corn, beans and squash. Many families fled to Fort Niagara and spent a terrible winter there. Washington became known among the Seneca as Hanodagá:nyas, Town Destroyer, a name remembered still.",
  "haudenosaunee:gather": "The Three Sisters, corn, beans and squash, grow together in the same mound: the corn gives the beans a pole to climb, the beans feed the soil, the squash shades the ground. Women owned the fields and directed the harvest, and a clan mother's word carried weight in council. At the Green Corn ceremony, the people give thanks for the ripening of the corn.",
  "haudenosaunee:ritual": "At midwinter, the Haudenosaunee hold their longest ceremony of the year, giving thanks and renewing the cycle. Dreams are shared and interpreted, for a dream could reveal what the soul needed. Medicine societies perform their work. The Thanksgiving Address is spoken, greeting the people, the earth, the waters, the plants, the animals, the winds, the moon, the sun, the stars and the Creator.",
  "haudenosaunee:rain": "Imagined: in a dry summer the corn leaves curl in the fields, and the elders gather at the longhouse to speak the Thanksgiving Address and give thanks to the Thunderers, the grandfathers who bring the rain. People wait, watching the western sky over the lake. When the first drops fall on the bark roof, the children run out into it.",
  "haudenosaunee:flood": "Imagined: spring snowmelt swells the Genesee River, flooding the low fields near a Seneca village. Families carry seed corn to higher ground. In the Haudenosaunee creation story, Sky Woman fell to a world of water, and animals dove to bring up mud, which they placed on the back of a turtle. The earth grew there: Turtle Island.",
  "haudenosaunee:wind": "In Haudenosaunee tradition, the wind spirits are honored in the Thanksgiving Address: the Four Winds, who bring the changing seasons and cleanse the air. Imagined: a great autumn storm off Lake Ontario tears at the elm bark of the longhouses, and families move to the center hearths, waiting it out together, the old people telling stories through the night.",
  "haudenosaunee:fire": "The Haudenosaunee Confederacy was founded when the Peacemaker and Hiawatha brought five warring nations together under the Great Tree of Peace. The central council fire burns at Onondaga, where the Grand Council still meets. To say the fire is lit means the council is in session. A fire in the longhouse was family, and the fire at Onondaga was the whole Confederacy.",
  "haudenosaunee:thunder": "The Thunderers, Hinon, are grandfathers who live in the west and bring the rain that waters the crops. They are honored in the Thanksgiving Address. In spring, ceremonies welcome back the Thunderers when their first rumbles are heard, thanking them for the coming rains and for keeping harmful creatures under the earth.",
  "haudenosaunee:birth": "Haudenosaunee society is matrilineal: a child belongs to its mother's clan, Turtle, Wolf, Bear or another, and clan mothers choose the chiefs. Decisions are to be made thinking of seven generations to come. Imagined: a child is born in a longhouse, and the clan mother holds it, wondering what this one will carry forward for the seventh generation.",
  "haudenosaunee:death": "When a chief dies, the Condolence Ceremony is held: the clearminded nations come to console the grieving nations, lifting their sorrow so they can see, hear and speak clearly again. The ceremony goes back to the Peacemaker, who comforted Hiawatha after he lost his daughters. Only then is a new chief raised up. Grief is shared by the whole Confederacy, not carried alone.",

  // ── Lakota ──
  "lakota:hunt": "Before the horse, the Lakota hunted buffalo on foot; after it, they became the great mounted hunters of the northern plains. A buffalo hunt was organized by akicita, the camp police, who kept anyone from charging early and scattering the herd. The tatanka gave meat, hides for tipis, horns for spoons, sinew for thread. Giving thanks was part of the hunt.",
  "lakota:battle": "On 25 June 1876, Lt. Col. George Custer attacked a huge camp of Lakota, Cheyenne and Arapaho on the Little Bighorn River, called the Greasy Grass. Warriors led by Crazy Horse, Gall and others counterattacked, and Custer and his whole battalion of over 200 men were killed. Sitting Bull had dreamed of soldiers falling into camp. It was the high point of resistance.",
  "lakota:gather": "Lakota women gathered tinpsila, wild prairie turnips, in early summer, digging them with a sharpened stick and braiding them to dry. They picked chokecherries in late summer, pounding them with buffalo meat and fat into wasna, pemmican, to last the winter. The moon of ripe chokecherries, Canpasapa Wi, is a time of plenty.",
  "lakota:ritual": "The Sun Dance, Wiwanyang Wacipi, is the most important Lakota ceremony, held in midsummer for the good of the people. Dancers make vows and offer their own suffering so that the people may live. The government banned it in 1883; it was held secretly and revived openly in the 20th century. A vision quest, hanbleceya, is crying for a vision, alone on a hill.",
  "lakota:rain": "In 1889 the Ghost Dance reached the Lakota from Wovoka, a Paiute prophet. He taught that if the people danced, the dead would return, the buffalo would come back, and the whites would vanish. Starving on reservations, their land cut by treaties, Lakota families danced and waited for the world to change. The government feared it, leading to tragedy.",
  "lakota:wind": "Imagined: a winter blizzard sweeps the plains, and the Lakota camp shelters in its tipis in a river valley lined with cottonwoods. The fire burns low, children sleep in buffalo robes, and grandmothers tell winter stories of Iktomi, the trickster spider. The winter count, a painted hide of years, will record this as the winter of the great snow.",
  "lakota:fire": "Imagined: in late summer a grass fire sweeps across the prairie, driven by the wind, faster than a horse can run. Scouts warn the camp, and families quickly take down tipis and move to the river, or set a back fire to burn a safe zone. Plains peoples knew how to use fire too, burning grass to bring fresh growth for the buffalo.",
  "lakota:thunder": "The Wakinyan, Thunder Beings, live in the west. A Lakota who dreams of them becomes a heyoka, a sacred clown who does everything backward, laughing when others mourn and shivering in summer. Black Elk, whose great vision came when he was nine, saw the Thunder Beings. Thunder is wakan, sacred, powerful and dangerous.",
  "lakota:birth": "In 1831, a boy was born on the Grand River and named Jumping Badger; as a young warrior he received his father's name, Tatanka Iyotake, Sitting Bull. Imagined: in a tipi on the plains, a mother gives birth while the grandmothers prepare a beaded turtle amulet for the umbilical cord. Will this child be a leader of the people? They pray to Wakan Tanka.",
  "lakota:death": "On 29 December 1890 at Wounded Knee Creek, soldiers of the 7th Cavalry opened fire on Big Foot's band of Miniconjou Lakota. Some 250 to 300 men, women and children were killed. The bodies lay frozen in the snow for days. Black Elk said: a people's dream died there. Today descendants hold the Big Foot Memorial Ride each December, a grief kept alive.",

  // ── Americans ──
  "americans:hunt": "In November 1902, President Theodore Roosevelt went bear hunting in Mississippi. His guides tied a black bear to a willow tree for him to shoot, and he refused, calling it unsportsmanlike. A cartoonist drew the scene; a Brooklyn shopkeeper made a stuffed bear and asked permission to call it Teddy's bear. Roosevelt also founded national forests and parks to protect what hunters loved.",
  "americans:battle": "Gettysburg, 1 to 3 July 1863. On the third day, some 12,500 Confederates make Pickett's Charge across an open field toward the Union center on Cemetery Ridge. Cannon and rifle fire cut them down; barely half return. Over three days, around 50,000 men are killed, wounded or missing. In November Lincoln spoke for two minutes: that this nation, under God, shall have a new birth of freedom.",
  "americans:gather": "In 1621 the Plymouth settlers, after their first harvest, held a three-day feast with some ninety Wampanoag men and their leader Massasoit, who brought five deer. Without Wampanoag help, especially Tisquantum, the colonists might not have survived. For many Native people, the fourth Thursday of November is now also a National Day of Mourning, held at Plymouth since 1970.",
  "americans:ritual": "Imagined: on the night before the 1969 Apollo 11 launch, families gather around televisions across America, many bowing their heads in prayer. On Christmas Eve 1968, the Apollo 8 crew, orbiting the Moon, had read from Genesis. Americans hoped for the future and feared the risk. For a country of many faiths, that night was a shared prayer.",
  "americans:rain": "In the 1930s, drought and poor farming turned the southern Plains into the Dust Bowl. On Black Sunday, 14 April 1935, a wall of dust rolled over Oklahoma and Texas, turning day into night. Families waited year after year for rain. Hundreds of thousands left for California. Woody Guthrie sang So Long, It's Been Good to Know Yuh, and Dorothea Lange photographed their faces.",
  "americans:flood": "On 31 May 1889 the South Fork Dam above Johnstown, Pennsylvania, failed after days of rain. A wall of water carrying trees, houses and railroad cars swept into the town, killing 2,209 people. The dam had belonged to a club of wealthy Pittsburgh industrialists. Clara Barton and the young Red Cross arrived and stayed five months, its first great relief effort.",
  "americans:wind": "On 29 August 2005, Hurricane Katrina struck the Gulf Coast. In New Orleans the levees failed, and 80 percent of the city flooded. People waited for days on rooftops and in the Superdome for help. More than 1,800 died. Musicians, chefs and families who scattered across the country came back to rebuild, and second-line parades returned to the streets.",
  "americans:fire": "On the night of 8 October 1871 a fire started in or near the O'Leary family's barn on DeKoven Street. Driven by strong winds through a city of wooden buildings, the Great Chicago Fire burned for two days, destroying over three square miles and leaving 100,000 homeless. The city rebuilt with stone, steel and ambition; the world's first skyscrapers rose there.",
  "americans:thunder": "In June 1752, the story goes, Benjamin Franklin flew a kite in a thunderstorm in Philadelphia with his son William, a key tied to the string. When the thread fibers stood up and the key sparked, he proved lightning was electricity. He then invented the lightning rod, and thunder over American cities became less frightening, a puzzle science had answered.",
  "americans:birth": "On 12 February 1809, in a one-room log cabin in Kentucky, Nancy Hanks Lincoln gave birth to a son, Abraham. She would die when he was nine. No one in that cabin imagined that he would lead the country through civil war and end slavery. Every American birth carries a little of that hope: anyone might become anything.",
  "americans:death": "In April 1865, after Lincoln's assassination, a funeral train carried his body 1,654 miles from Washington to Springfield, Illinois, retracing his journey to the presidency. Millions stood by the tracks, many at night with bonfires, to watch it pass. In New Orleans today, jazz funerals march with a brass band, slow hymns going to the grave and joyful music coming back.",

  // ── Mexicans ──
  "mexicans:hunt": "Imagined: in the Sierra Tarahumara, Rarámuri runners hunt deer the old way, by running them down over hours until the animal tires. On the haciendas of the north, charros rope cattle in the charreada, the national sport born from ranch work. Hunting and herding in Mexico mix Indigenous endurance with the horsemanship brought from Spain.",
  "mexicans:battle": "On 5 May 1862, at Puebla, General Ignacio Zaragoza's poorly equipped Mexican army defeated the French expeditionary force, then reputed the finest army in the world. The French charged uphill against the forts of Loreto and Guadalupe in the rain and were thrown back. France later took Mexico City, but Cinco de Mayo became a symbol that Mexico could stand up to empires.",
  "mexicans:gather": "In the milpa, Mexican farmers plant maize, beans and squash together, as their ancestors did for thousands of years; Mexico gave the world maize, chocolate, chili, tomatoes and vanilla. Each region has its own maize, blue, red, black, white. Women grind nixtamal for tortillas at dawn. In the 1940s Norman Borlaug's wheat research in Sonora began the Green Revolution.",
  "mexicans:ritual": "On 12 December, millions of pilgrims arrive at the Basilica of Guadalupe in Mexico City, many on their knees, to honor the Virgin who, the story says, appeared to Juan Diego on the hill of Tepeyac in 1531, speaking Nahuatl. Mariachis sing Las Mañanitas at midnight. People ask her for health, work, safe crossing and their children's future.",
  "mexicans:rain": "In 1910 Mexico waited for change under Porfirio Díaz, who had ruled for over thirty years. Francisco Madero called for revolution on 20 November. In the south Emiliano Zapata rose with peasants demanding land: Tierra y Libertad. The waiting broke into a decade of revolution that killed perhaps a million people and remade Mexico.",
  "mexicans:flood": "In the colonial period Mexico City, built on a drained lake, flooded again and again. In 1629 a great flood left the city underwater for five years; thousands died, and many moved away. The Spanish dug the Huehuetoca drain to empty the valley, work that continued for centuries. Today the city is still sinking as its aquifers are pumped dry.",
  "mexicans:wind": "In October 2023 Hurricane Otis grew in less than a day from a tropical storm to a Category 5 hurricane, striking Acapulco without warning. Hotels and homes were torn open, boats sunk in the bay. Families waited for help in the ruins. Acapulquenses, proud of their port, began clearing the streets the next morning.",
  "mexicans:fire": "Every year in Mexico, on the night before Easter, Judas effigies made of papier-mâché, packed with fireworks, are burned in the streets. In Tultepec, the fireworks capital, the Feria de la Pirotecnia in March honors San Juan de Dios with toritos, bulls of fireworks, charging through crowds. Fire in Mexico is celebration and danger together.",
  "mexicans:thunder": "On 19 September 1985 at 7:19 in the morning, a magnitude 8.0 earthquake struck Mexico City with a roar like thunder. Buildings collapsed; thousands died. The government was slow, so neighbors organized rescue brigades themselves; the topos dug through rubble by hand. On the same date in 2017, after a drill, another earthquake struck, and the brigades came out again.",
  "mexicans:birth": "Imagined: in a family in Oaxaca, a baby girl is born, and her grandmother wraps her in a rebozo and lights a candle to the Virgin of Guadalupe. Will she be a doctor, an artist like Frida Kahlo, or will she go north for work? The family plans her baptism and, years later, her quinceañera, and hopes.",
  "mexicans:death": "On 1 and 2 November, for Día de Muertos, families build ofrendas with marigolds, candles, sugar skulls, pan de muerto and the favorite foods of the dead. In Pátzcuaro and Mixquic they keep vigil in the cemeteries all night, with music and stories. The marigold petals guide the souls home. Death is not only grief here: it is a reunion.",

  // ── Mapuche ──
  "mapuche:hunt": "Imagined: in the forests of Araucanía, a Mapuche hunter goes after pudú, the tiny deer, and guanaco on the slopes, carrying bolas, the weighted throwing cords. Before he enters the forest he asks permission of the ngen, the spirit owners of the woods and waters. In the araucaria forests, families gather piñones, the great pine nuts.",
  "mapuche:battle": "On 25 December 1553, the Mapuche toqui Lautaro, who had served as Pedro de Valdivia's groom and learned Spanish tactics, led an attack on the fort of Tucapel. Valdivia was captured and killed. The Mapuche resisted the Spanish for over three centuries; the Biobío River became the border. Alonso de Ercilla's epic La Araucana honored their courage.",
  "mapuche:gather": "Imagined: in autumn, Mapuche families climb the Andean slopes to gather piñones from the araucaria trees, a staple that can be eaten boiled, roasted or ground into flour. The trees, pewen, are sacred, and the Pehuenche, the people of the pewen, are named after them. Children play under the great trees while elders sort the nuts.",
  "mapuche:ritual": "The Nguillatun is the great Mapuche community prayer, held to ask Ngenechen for good harvests, rain, health and balance. The community gathers around a rewe, a carved post, with offerings. The machi, a spiritual healer, plays the kultrun drum and climbs the rewe in trance. Many families take part in prayer over several days.",
  "mapuche:rain": "Imagined: after years of drought in southern Chile, a Mapuche community near Temuco, its rivers diminished by drought and plantations of pine and eucalyptus, gathers for a Nguillatun to ask for rain. Elders recall when the rivers ran full. They wait, praying, watching the clouds come from the Pacific.",
  "mapuche:flood": "Mapuche tradition tells of an ancient flood: Kai Kai Vilu, the sea serpent, raised the waters to drown the people, and Treng Treng Vilu, the serpent of the land, raised the hills so they could climb to safety. On 22 May 1960 the strongest earthquake ever recorded, magnitude 9.5, struck near Valdivia, and a tsunami swept the coast. Many Mapuche saw the old struggle of the serpents once more.",
  "mapuche:wind": "Imagined: a winter storm from the Pacific sweeps over the Araucanía, bending the forests and tearing at the ruka, the thatched houses. Families gather around the central fire, which never goes out, and the elders tell epew, the old stories. The wind is one of the forces that the Mapuche recognize as alive.",
  "mapuche:fire": "Imagined: summer wildfires, fed by drought and pine plantations, sweep through southern Chile. A Mapuche community watches its forest and fields burn and its sacred places threatened. Young people form brigades and go into the smoke to protect homes and the elderly. The fire brings to the surface a long struggle over land.",
  "mapuche:thunder": "Imagined: thunder echoes off the volcanoes Villarrica and Llaima, and Mapuche elders say the pillan, the spirits of the volcanoes and ancestors, are speaking. The pillan is honored and feared, associated with thunder, lightning and eruptions. A machi may be consulted to understand what the storm means for the community.",
  "mapuche:birth": "Imagined: a baby is born in a Mapuche ruka in Araucanía, and the grandmother speaks to it first in Mapudungun, the language of the land. The family remembers Lautaro, born around 1534, a boy taken by the Spanish who returned to lead his people. Some hope this child will defend the land; others only pray it grows up healthy and free.",
  "mapuche:death": "When a Mapuche person dies, the community holds an eluwün, a funeral that may last several days. People gather, speak of the dead, and share food. The body is placed in a canoe-shaped coffin, wampo, and carried to the cemetery, and the grave may be marked with a chemamüll, a carved wooden figure. The dead journey west across the sea.",

  // ── Muisca ──
  "muisca:hunt": "Imagined: on the high plateau of Bogotá, Muisca hunters chase white-tailed deer and capture guinea pigs and ducks around the lakes. Deer hunting was restricted, some say reserved for the nobility. The hunters carry spear throwers and nets, and their village chief takes a share. The meat is shared at feasts with chicha, the maize beer.",
  "muisca:battle": "In 1537 the Spanish conquistador Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada climbed into the Muisca highlands from the Magdalena River with his exhausted men. The zipa Tisquesusa fought, but was killed in 1538. The zaque Quemuenchatocha at Hunza was taken. The Muisca confederation, rich in gold and salt, fell within two years.",
  "muisca:gather": "The Muisca were famous for salt, mined at Zipaquirá and Nemocón by boiling brine in clay pots. Salt cakes were traded across Colombia, with emeralds, cotton cloth and gold. Muisca farmers grew maize, potatoes and quinoa on terraced fields and raised beds. The harvest and the salt trade made them one of the richest peoples of the Andes.",
  "muisca:ritual": "When a new zipa was made, the Muisca performed a ceremony at Lake Guatavita: the heir, covered in gold dust, was rowed on a raft to the middle of the lake, where he and his companions offered gold and emeralds to the waters. This ceremony inspired the legend of El Dorado. The Muisca Raft, found in 1969, shows it in gold.",
  "muisca:rain": "The Muisca waited for the rains and watched the sky, and their priests, the chyquy, kept a calendar based on moon and sun. Imagined: during a long dry season, the people of Bacatá go in procession to a sacred lake, carrying offerings of tunjos, small gold figures, to ask for rain. The priests fast and watch for signs.",
  "muisca:flood": "In Muisca tradition, the god Chibchacum, angry at the people, flooded the plateau of Bogotá. The people prayed to Bochica, who appeared on a rainbow and struck the rocks with his golden staff, opening a gap for the water to flow out: the Tequendama Falls. Chibchacum was condemned to carry the world on his shoulders; when he shifts it, the earth trembles.",
  "muisca:wind": "Imagined: cold winds sweep over the páramo, the high moorland above the Muisca villages, carrying mist and rain. Families stay inside their round houses, weaving cotton mantas by the fire. The páramo lakes were sacred, and the wind that came down from them was respected, a messenger of the high places.",
  "muisca:fire": "Sogamoso, called Sugamuxi, held the great Temple of the Sun of the Muisca. In 1537, Spanish soldiers looting it by torchlight are said to have set it on fire accidentally; it burned for days. A rebuilt version stands today in the archaeological museum of Sogamoso. For the Muisca it was the loss of their holiest place.",
  "muisca:thunder": "Imagined: thunder rolls over the Bogotá plateau, and the Muisca say it is the gods' voice. Bochica, the culture hero, had taught them weaving and law and given them the rainbow. After a storm, a rainbow, Cuchavira, was taken as a sign of his protection. Women in labor and the sick prayed to Cuchavira.",
  "muisca:birth": "Imagined: in a Muisca village, a woman gives birth and the family makes offerings to Bachué, the mother goddess who rose from Lake Iguaque with a child in her arms and populated the earth. Will this child be a cacique, a priest trained for years in seclusion, or a salt maker? The family offers a small gold tunjo.",
  "muisca:death": "Muisca nobles were mummified, dried and wrapped in fine cotton mantas, and placed in caves or temples with gold and emeralds. Some were carried into battle to give courage. Mourners sang laments for days, and chicha was drunk in their memory. Ordinary people were buried with their tools and pots, ready for the life to come.",

  // ── Tupi ──
  "tupi:hunt": "Tupi hunters along the Brazilian coast moved quietly through the Atlantic forest with bows taller than themselves, hunting tapir, peccary and monkeys. Jean de Léry, a Frenchman who lived among the Tupinambá in 1557, described their skill with the bow and the joy with which they returned to the village, sharing the meat with everyone.",
  "tupi:battle": "In 1554, Hans Staden, a German gunner, was captured by the Tupinambá near São Vicente. He was told he would be eaten in a ritual, the fate of a respected enemy. He wrote that he survived nine months by his wits and prayers until a French ship ransomed him. His book, published in 1557, describes the warfare and rituals of the Tupinambá in vivid detail.",
  "tupi:gather": "Each year Tupi families cut and burned a patch of forest to plant manioc, maize and beans; the ash fertilizes the soil, and after a few years the forest returns. Fire here is ordinary life, not disaster. Women grate the manioc and squeeze out its poison in long woven tubes, the tipiti, to make flour, farinha, still eaten across Brazil.",
  "tupi:ritual": "The Tupi pajé, the shaman, traveled between villages and spoke with the spirits, shaking a gourd rattle, the maracá, while tobacco smoke filled the air. Villagers asked whether the war would go well, whether the harvest would be good. Jesuit missionaries like José de Anchieta wrote in Tupi and tried to replace the pajés; the Tupi language survived as Brazil's lingua franca for centuries.",
  "tupi:rain": "Some Tupi-Guarani peoples migrated in search of Yvy Marã Ey, the Land Without Evil, a place without death or suffering. Prophets, the karaí, led whole villages on long journeys toward the sea or the west, waiting for the world to change. Movements continued into the 20th century among the Guarani. The hope was not for weather, but for a new world.",
  "tupi:flood": "In a Tupinambá myth recorded by the French friar André Thevet in the 1550s, a great flood covered the earth, and only two brothers and their wives survived by climbing a tall palm tree. From them came the Tupinambá and their rivals. The flood was the beginning of the peoples, and of their enmity.",
  "tupi:wind": "Imagined: a summer storm sweeps in from the Atlantic, and the palm-thatched longhouses of a Tupinambá village shake in the wind. Hammocks, a Tupi invention the world borrowed, swing. Families gather around the fires, children cling to their mothers, and the old men say it is the voice of Tupã, the thunder.",
  "tupi:fire": "In September, at the end of the dry season, a Tupi village burns its new field. Men felled the trees months earlier; now the dry wood is set alight, and smoke rises above the forest as the whole village watches. The ash will feed the manioc. Slash-and-burn is ordinary life, the way the forest feeds the people, and after a few years they move on and the forest returns.",
  "tupi:thunder": "Tupã is the voice of the thunder for the Tupi-Guarani peoples. When Jesuit missionaries arrived, they used Tupã as the word for God, and it remains so in the Guarani language today. Imagined: a child in a coastal village hears thunder for the first time and runs to her grandmother, who tells her it is Tupã, passing over the forest.",
  "tupi:birth": "Jean de Léry wrote that when a Tupinambá child was born, the father cut the umbilical cord with his teeth and then lay in his hammock for days, resting and fasting, receiving visitors, as if he had given birth himself, the custom called couvade. The child received a name, sometimes that of an ancestor or a respected enemy.",
  "tupi:death": "Jean de Léry, who lived among the Tupinambá in 1557, saw the women wail for hours over the dead, recounting his deeds: how well he hunted, how bravely he fought. The body was bent into a seated position and buried in a round pit, sometimes inside a large clay urn, with food and belongings. For days afterward food was left, and fires kept, to keep the spirit Aignan away.",
});
